---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-to-calculate-hours-worked"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    Calculating hours worked is subtraction plus one conversion: elapsed time
    between clock-in and clock-out, minus unpaid breaks, converted to decimal
    hours for payroll. Here is the method, with the parts that trip people up.
  </p>

  <h2>The four steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Write both times in the same format.</strong> Convert to a 24-hour clock if that is easier: 1:30 PM becomes 13:30.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtract the start time from the end time.</strong> 8:00 to 16:30 is 8 hours 30 minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtract unpaid breaks.</strong> A 30-minute unpaid lunch leaves 8 hours 0 minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Convert the minutes to a decimal</strong> by dividing by 60. 30 ÷ 60 = 0.5, so the day is 8.00 hours.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><code>hours worked = (end − start) − unpaid break, then minutes ÷ 60</code></p>

  <h2>Overnight shifts</h2>
  <p>
    When the end time is earlier than the start time, the shift crossed
    midnight. Add 24 hours before subtracting. A shift from 22:00 to 06:00 is
    (06:00 + 24:00) − 22:00 = 8 hours.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes to decimal — the common mistake</h2>
  <p>
    7 hours 45 minutes is <strong>7.75</strong>, not 7.45. The minutes are a
    fraction of 60, not of 100.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Decimal</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>15</td><td>0.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20</td><td>0.33</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30</td><td>0.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>45</td><td>0.75</td></tr>
      <tr><td>50</td><td>0.83</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The <a href="/calculators/decimal-hours-calculator/">Decimal Hours Calculator</a> has the full 0–59 chart and a bulk converter.</p>

  <h2>Rounding</h2>
  <p>
    Many employers round each punch to the nearest quarter hour or tenth of an
    hour. In the US, rounding is allowed under the Fair Labor Standards Act only
    if it does not consistently favour the employer over time. The well-known
    "7-minute rule" rounds 1–7 minutes down and 8–14 minutes up to the nearest
    quarter hour. Record your actual times; round only if your workplace policy
    says to.
  </p>

  <h2>A full week</h2>
  <p>
    Add the daily decimal totals. Overtime, where it applies, is usually
    calculated on the weekly total (over 40 hours under US federal law, over 44
    in Ontario). See <a href="/guides/how-to-calculate-overtime-pay/">how to calculate overtime pay</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Let a calculator do it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/">Hours Worked Calculator</a> — one shift or a split shift, with a break.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> — a full week, with overtime and gross pay.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/time-clock-calculator/">Time Clock Calculator</a> — a list of clock-in/out punches for payroll.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
